🚀 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟬/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗹𝗼𝘆𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗙𝗮𝗶𝗹𝗲𝗱 𝗔𝗳𝘁𝗲𝗿 𝗮 𝗥𝗲𝗰𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗻𝗴𝗲 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗗𝗼?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “A new application version was deployed to production, but users started facing errors immediately after the deployment. How will you troubleshoot and recover?”

I wouldn't start making multiple changes at once.

First, I would confirm the impact and identify what changed.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check deployment status

kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name>

2️⃣ Check pods

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Look for:

🔹 CrashLoopBackOff
🔹 ImagePullBackOff
🔹 Failed probes
🔹 Increased restarts
🔹 Pending pods

3️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

If the pod restarted:

kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous -n <namespace>

4️⃣ Check what changed

I would compare the current deployment with the previous working version:

🔹 Container image
🔹 Environment variables
🔹 ConfigMaps/Secrets
🔹 Resource requests/limits
🔹 Application configuration

5️⃣ Check rollout history

kubectl rollout history deployment/<deployment-name>

If the new release is confirmed as the cause and rollback is approved:

kubectl rollout undo deployment/<deployment-name>

Then verify:

kubectl rollout status deployment/<deployment-name>

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Impact → Deployment → Pods → Logs → Recent Changes → Root Cause → Recovery → Verification

After recovery, I would monitor the application and document the incident:

Issue → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A rollback is a recovery action, not a root-cause analysis.

The goal is not just to restore service, but also to understand why the new release caused the issue and prevent it from happening again. 🚀

